---

Why this licence, and how to change it

A public repository with no LICENSE file is legally "all rights reserved"
but is widely read as "help yourself". This file removes that ambiguity in
the more conservative direction, because the choice is asymmetric: an
all-rights-reserved project can be opened up later at any time, whereas an
MIT or Apache grant cannot be withdrawn from code already released under
it. Keeping the option open costs nothing today.

To open this up later, replace this file with the full text of the licence
you choose (MIT and Apache-2.0 are the usual candidates; Apache-2.0 also
carries an express patent grant) and note the change in README.md.
